## Chapter 07: Switch Statements

![Handwritten-style study note about JavaScript switch statements](assets/images/notes-switch.svg)

Use `switch` when one value needs to be compared against several cases. `break` prevents execution from falling through to the next case, while `default` handles values with no match.

Lessons: [`50_switch.js`](07_chapter_switch/50_switch.js), [`51_IQ.js`](07_chapter_switch/51_IQ.js), [`52_IQ.js`](07_chapter_switch/52_IQ.js), [`53_API_Switch.js`](07_chapter_switch/53_API_Switch.js), [`54_Switch_group.js`](07_chapter_switch/54_Switch_group.js), [`55_IQ.js`](07_chapter_switch/55_IQ.js), [`56_IQ.js`](07_chapter_switch/56_IQ.js), [`57_IQ.js`](07_chapter_switch/57_IQ.js), and [`58_IQ.js`](07_chapter_switch/58_IQ.js).

---

## Chapter 08: User Input

![Handwritten-style study note about reading user input in Node.js](assets/images/notes-user-input.svg)

These examples explore prompt-based input and Node.js terminal input with `readline`, `prompt-sync`, and the filesystem API. In [`62_FS_readfile.js`](08_chapter_user_input/62_FS_readfile.js), `readFileSync(0, "utf8")` reads standard input until end-of-file. Run it with `node 08_chapter_user_input/62_FS_readfile.js`, type a value, then press `Ctrl+D` on macOS/Linux (or `Ctrl+Z`, then Enter, on Windows) to finish input.

Lessons: [`59_UI.js`](08_chapter_user_input/59_UI.js), [`60_node_Ui.js`](08_chapter_user_input/60_node_Ui.js), [`61_Prompt_sync.js`](08_chapter_user_input/61_Prompt_sync.js), and [`62_FS_readfile.js`](08_chapter_user_input/62_FS_readfile.js).

